{
  "dom_id": "step-e689",
  "name": "Example: Determining Whether a Geometric Series Given in Sigma Notation Converges",
  "problem": "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n+1}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
